00:06:14,663 --> 00:06:17,575
train the model based on historical data.

95
00:06:17,935 --> 00:06:21,784
And on the other side, you have
the online store component, which

96
00:06:21,784 --> 00:06:23,290
is used for real-time inference.

97
00:06:23,299 --> 00:06:26,049
So the online store is
going to be very fast.

98
00:06:26,069 --> 00:06:31,520
It's going to be directly connected
to the user, interface when it gets

99
00:06:31,540 --> 00:06:34,123
predicted the next content to show

100
00:06:35,123 --> 00:06:37,975
So a little bit about the feature stores.

101
00:06:37,994 --> 00:06:40,577
Why would you want to use a feature store?

102
00:06:41,385 --> 00:06:45,042
at a bigger company, you
might have many people or many

103
00:06:45,042 --> 00:06:46,860
teams that work on features.

104
00:06:47,007 --> 00:06:51,647
one team might build a feature that
another team already built, but because

105
00:06:51,648 --> 00:06:57,300
they can't see each other's work, they,
they basically do the same work twice.

106
00:06:57,306 --> 00:07:02,169
So with a central feature store, you build
a feature once and every model and every

107
00:07:02,169 --> 00:07:05,236
team across the company can reuse it.

108
00:07:07,180 --> 00:07:11,091
another big benefit is,
the self-serve aspect.

109
00:07:11,270 --> 00:07:15,415
So people who create the features,
let's say data scientists, they don't

110
00:07:15,421 --> 00:07:20,114
need, an engineer or engineering team
to be able to ship those features

111
00:07:20,122 --> 00:07:23,874
that they created because the whole
infrastructure is already in place and

112
00:07:23,874 --> 00:07:28,202
they can just push new features, when
they, when they have them ready or

113
00:07:28,205 --> 00:07:29,799
when they want to add the new feature.

114
00:07:31,344 --> 00:07:34,565
one pipeline, so again, I mentioned
offline and online stores.

115
00:07:34,570 --> 00:07:38,679
With a feature store, you centralize both
of those things, which is also a huge

116
00:07:38,694 --> 00:07:43,046
benefit that you don't need to maintain
two separate things for training and

117
00:07:43,077 --> 00:07:49,789
real-time inference So what is the main
difference between these two workloads?

118
00:07:50,560 --> 00:07:54,046
So for real-time inference, I
mentioned, it needs to be fast.

119
00:07:54,284 --> 00:07:58,727
In practice, you usually have very
strict, SLAs in terms of the latency

120
00:07:59,076 --> 00:08:01,872
requirement for specific queries.

121
00:08:01,882 --> 00:08:07,479
So you might say that you need ten
millisecond latency here in order to make

122
00:08:07,479 --> 00:08:10,316
the experience good on the user's, side.

123
00:08:11,778 --> 00:08:15,189
In terms of model training, you
don't have, you don't have this

124
00:08:15,207 --> 00:08:16,546
kind of latency requirement.

125
00:08:16,555 --> 00:08:20,889
Model training takes longer, it's expected
to take longer, and it's not user-facing.

126
00:08:21,882 --> 00:08:22,733
So that's a big…

127
00:08:22,935 --> 00:08:26,685
probably the biggest difference
between the two workloads in terms

128
00:08:26,685 --> 00:08:28,309
of the database requirements.

129
00:08:29,309 --> 00:08:34,799
one thing I want to highlight here about
this slide is that, while you are--

130
00:08:35,005 --> 00:08:36,838
Like, what are you querying here, right?

131
00:08:36,838 --> 00:08:40,085
Because for model training,
you are querying a huge

132
00:08:40,085 --> 00:08:42,096
dataset, like billions of rows.

133
00:08:42,815 --> 00:08:46,124
on the other hand, with
real-time inference, you are not

134
00:08:46,131 --> 00:08:47,374
querying a lot of data usually.

135
00:08:47,374 --> 00:08:51,278
You are query, querying one
feature vector, which, could

136
00:08:51,279 --> 00:08:52,796
be multiple rows, right?

137
00:08:52,833 --> 00:08:56,306
which could be, I don't know, thousands
of rows, tens of thousands of rows.

138
00:08:56,643 --> 00:09:00,498
But it's a small, a very small,
portion of the overall dataset.

139
00:09:00,775 --> 00:09:02,542
But it needs to be very fast.

140
00:09:02,544 --> 00:09:06,514
It needs to be available,
accessible, under very strict

141
00:09:06,543 --> 00:09:09,957
P99, latency or requirements.

142
00:09:10,543 --> 00:09:15,865
Now, why is latency so important, and
why are we talking about literally

143
00:09:15,867 --> 00:09:19,483
milliseconds or even sub-milliseconds
in some cases when it comes to

144
00:09:20,924 --> 00:09:22,855
feature stores and online stores?

145
00:09:24,127 --> 00:09:30,075
if, if you look at this from the user
side, the user, let's say, does one thing.

146
00:09:30,086 --> 00:09:34,755
It may be it swipes or it opens
a page or refreshes the page.

147
00:09:34,967 --> 00:09:36,965
So that is one action the user takes.

148
00:09:37,325 --> 00:09:42,927
But under the hood, it's gonna turn
into multiple API calls on the back

149
00:09:42,927 --> 00:09:48,429
end, and each of those services will
probably make a bunch of database calls.

150
00:09:48,473 --> 00:09:53,945
So even though the user only took one
action, that one action will turn into

151
00:09:53,947 --> 00:09:58,857
many database calls, on the database
server, and the database has to serve

152
00:09:58,871 --> 00:10:01,862
those requests at the same time.

153
00:10:02,360 --> 00:10:06,211
And so what ends up happening in
practice is that the page that the

154
00:10:06,212 --> 00:10:12,229
user sees is only as fast as the
slowest, of those database lookups.

155
00:10:13,406 --> 00:10:17,442
So one way to illustrate this is, is
actually, there's this paper that was

156
00:10:17,453 --> 00:10:22,633
produced, a while ago, about tail latency,
because what you are monitoring in

157
00:10:22,645 --> 00:10:28,387
low-latency applications is usually the
tail latency, whether that's P95, P99.

158
00:10:28,702 --> 00:10:31,472
I've seen some discussion around
P, one hundred, monitoring

159
00:10:31,473 --> 00:10:34,202
P100, which is interesting.

160
00:10:34,597 --> 00:10:40,419
but basically, you're measuring tail
latency to be able to, minimize the

161
00:10:40,433 --> 00:10:46,271
overall, latency that the average user
experiences or most users experience.

162
00:10:48,186 --> 00:10:52,284
so first of all, what is
P50, P99, P9, P95, P99?

163
00:10:52,286 --> 00:10:53,812
And so these are percentiles.

164
00:10:54,707 --> 00:10:59,194
And so for example, in the case of P50,
that is going to be the med-med-median.

165
00:10:59,533 --> 00:11:06,334
So half of the requests, are going
to be slower than the P50, value.

166
00:11:07,723 --> 00:11:10,681
so in a sense, it's one in, 20.

167
00:11:10,726 --> 00:11:12,993
One in, 20 is going to be slower.

168
00:11:13,400 --> 00:11:18,138
In the case of P99, one in
hundred is going to be slower,

169
00:11:18,202 --> 00:11:19,591
so that's the tail latency.

170
00:11:20,121 --> 00:11:26,797
And so now if you, … Le- let's say we
want to do 100 lookups, then each lookup

171
00:11:27,237 --> 00:11:30,981
has a 99% chance to be faster than P99.

172
00:11:31,786 --> 00:11:37,153
The chance that all
hundred are fast is, 0.99.

173
00:11:38,423 --> 00:11:44,514
And so if you do the math, at
the end, 63% of page loads wait

174
00:11:44,523 --> 00:11:46,823
for at least one slow lookup.

175
00:11:47,049 --> 00:11:51,662
And so one slow query out of
hundred slows down the whole page.

176
00:11:52,645 --> 00:11:56,854
And m- I recommend reading the Tail
Latency at Scale, research paper,

177
00:11:56,854 --> 00:12:01,053
which I think it was, created,
produced, by Google, a, a while ago.

178
00:12:01,976 --> 00:12:08,293
but, yeah, that's why P99 is very
important because, if it's unpredictable

179
00:12:08,586 --> 00:12:15,142
or it's not below, below a certain,
SLA, then it can cause problems on

180
00:12:15,148 --> 00:12:21,531
the overall infrastructure, and it
can lead to a bad user experience.

181
00:12:21,542 --> 00:12:25,830
So you often have very strict
SLAs about the tail latency.

182
00:12:26,830 --> 00:12:30,700
Now, with databases and storage,
you always have to make trade-offs.

183
00:12:30,725 --> 00:12:35,169
One of these trade-offs is,
related to latency, low latency

184
00:12:35,216 --> 00:12:37,851
and the, cost of storage.

185
00:12:37,852 --> 00:12:43,580
So usually, if you want, less expensive
storage, you won't get low latency.

186
00:12:43,651 --> 00:12:47,243
So you have to decide what you need,
what you're willing to give up.

187
00:12:47,472 --> 00:12:49,487
In a feature store,
you actually need both.

188
00:12:49,622 --> 00:12:55,887
You need cheap storage for storing
training data, storing large data sets,

189
00:12:55,913 --> 00:13:01,215
petabytes of data for training, and
you also need low latency storage for

190
00:13:01,215 --> 00:13:07,245
real-time serving an interesting, thing
about this is that you are-- you usually

191
00:13:07,245 --> 00:13:12,376
store much more data in your offline store
than your online store, which makes sense.

192
00:13:12,527 --> 00:13:14,351
This example is from, TripAdvisor.

193
00:13:14,644 --> 00:13:18,377
they have or at least they used to
have, about one hundred and twenty-five

194
00:13:18,391 --> 00:13:20,460
terabytes of data in their offline store.

195
00:13:20,776 --> 00:13:24,330
Again, that is datasets
for training purposes.

196
00:13:25,482 --> 00:13:31,596
And, they store nine terabytes in
their online store, meaning ScyllaDB.

197
00:13:32,383 --> 00:13:36,588
And so there's a big difference, big
difference in the amount of data that,

198
00:13:36,677 --> 00:13:41,962
you are supposed to store in these two,
places All right, I want to talk about

199
00:13:41,962 --> 00:13:47,509
data modeling because, if we talk about
low latency performance, data modeling

200
00:13:48,255 --> 00:13:50,734
is going to play a huge role in that.

201
00:13:51,717 --> 00:13:55,684
And, even if you choose, like
theoret-theoretically, let's say you

202
00:13:55,684 --> 00:13:59,149
choose the right database for the
job, if you have a bad data model,

203
00:13:59,261 --> 00:14:04,048
you still won't get the performance
and you won't be able to, meet the…

204
00:14:04,062 --> 00:14:06,237
meet strict, SLA requirements.

205
00:14:06,241 --> 00:14:09,140
So let's talk a little bit about this.

206
00:14:10,104 --> 00:14:15,792
So to start off, I just wanna compare
different ways to create data models.

207
00:14:15,803 --> 00:14:21,140
So there are, I'm gonna focus on two
ways right now, one that works with,

208
00:14:21,200 --> 00:14:26,191
relational databases and another one that
works with NoSQL databases like ScyllaDB.

209
00:14:26,649 --> 00:14:32,038
So relational data modeling starts
usually with entities and, you look

210
00:14:32,038 --> 00:14:36,369
at your domain, you look at the m-
the model, the relations in your data,

211
00:14:36,575 --> 00:14:42,664
and you create normalized tables, and
finally, you write the, the SQL queries.

212
00:14:42,683 --> 00:14:45,343
Maybe you also need to join tables.

213
00:14:46,414 --> 00:14:51,727
So for low latency applications, this
approach is not going to be, good enough.

214
00:14:51,892 --> 00:14:54,501
For low latency, you need to
have a different approach.

215
00:14:54,501 --> 00:14:57,461
You need to start from the application.

216
00:14:57,472 --> 00:15:01,224
So in practice, you start from the
application, you figure out what data

217
00:15:01,224 --> 00:15:06,737
you need to fetch, on a given page,
and then you write those queries.

218
00:15:06,846 --> 00:15:09,575
And then based on those
queries, you design the tables.

219
00:15:09,586 --> 00:15:11,944
So it's a query first approach.

220
00:15:11,946 --> 00:15:17,337
You design tables for specific queries,
maybe even one table per query,

221
00:15:18,553 --> 00:15:20,034
so you hit the correct partition.

222
00:15:20,483 --> 00:15:22,423
you might also denormalize data.

223
00:15:22,614 --> 00:15:25,895
So let's say you have a table
that serves a specific query.

224
00:15:25,895 --> 00:15:30,170
You have a similar query, but slightly
different with different like filtering.

225
00:15:30,605 --> 00:15:35,678
in the where clause you cannot use the
same table, so you create another table.

226
00:15:35,679 --> 00:15:39,417
You might, use some of the features
that are available in your database,

227
00:15:39,442 --> 00:15:43,969
materialized views, indexes, but under
the hood, you're gonna create new

228
00:15:44,007 --> 00:15:49,164
tables, you're gonna duplicate data, and
you're doing this to achieve really good

229
00:15:49,337 --> 00:15:55,491
performance Now let's see what it would
look like in a feature store scenario

230
00:15:55,505 --> 00:16:00,964
for a feature store use case, how you
can define, how you can design schemas.

231
00:16:02,051 --> 00:16:05,714
So here's an example, published
by, Medium's feature store.

232
00:16:06,628 --> 00:16:08,500
it holds, list, features.

233
00:16:09,373 --> 00:16:14,982
And so the entity is the user, and
the feature, is, reading history

234
00:16:15,151 --> 00:16:20,523
and behold, things like, story IDs,
wh- and, when that story was, read.

235
00:16:21,326 --> 00:16:26,242
So one thing that you notice, with this is
that it's a, it's a narrow table design.

236
00:16:26,242 --> 00:16:27,548
We don't have a lot of columns.

237
00:16:27,924 --> 00:16:31,821
usually if you build feature stores,
you're not gonna have a lot of columns.

238
00:16:32,126 --> 00:16:37,802
You might decide to have, tens
or hundreds of different columns

239
00:16:37,823 --> 00:16:43,209
if you create a design where each
column will hold one feature.

240
00:16:43,807 --> 00:16:48,211
But, in practice, in my experience,
that's not what companies do because

241
00:16:48,212 --> 00:16:52,068
it doesn't make sense because that's
gonna be, not very easy to modify.

242
00:16:52,096 --> 00:16:57,043
In practice, you always add new features,
you remove features, you, you change how

243
00:16:57,044 --> 00:17:03,144
you work with features, and this kind of
narrow schema allows you to keep changing

244
00:17:03,144 --> 00:17:06,948
the features because if a feature, Let's
say you drop a feature, you're not…

245
00:17:06,979 --> 00:17:09,284
You don't need to modify, the…

246
00:17:09,284 --> 00:17:10,680
you don't need to alter the table.

247
00:17:10,680 --> 00:17:12,281
You don't need to run migrations.

248
00:17:12,583 --> 00:17:18,843
In the case of a wide, wide table
design where you have, a lot of

249
00:17:18,843 --> 00:17:22,187
columns, you, you will need to keep
altering the table, altering the

250
00:17:22,188 --> 00:17:23,919
schema when you change the feature.

251
00:17:23,922 --> 00:17:26,559
So this seems to be the better option.

252
00:17:27,009 --> 00:17:31,386
And, usually you have, a column
where you store, some ID re-

253
00:17:31,398 --> 00:17:33,437
in, re- related to the entity.

254
00:17:33,747 --> 00:17:39,234
So you have some sort of an entity ID
y- and you need to store the value.

255
00:17:39,758 --> 00:17:41,157
or sorry, the, the feature key.

256
00:17:41,169 --> 00:17:45,047
So in the, in, in this case, it's
called a feature key, or you can

257
00:17:45,047 --> 00:17:46,475
also call it the feature name.

258
00:17:46,897 --> 00:17:49,883
It's going to be the name of the
feature and then the value which

259
00:17:49,885 --> 00:17:52,049
is going to be the actual, value.

260
00:17:52,065 --> 00:17:55,860
In this case, we are using text as
the data type to store all of this.

261
00:17:56,313 --> 00:17:59,154
but there are other implementations
as well, as you will see later,

262
00:17:59,369 --> 00:18:03,243
where you are using, for example,
b- for example, blob to store the

263
00:18:03,257 --> 00:18:05,465
serialized, version of the value.

264
00:18:06,402 --> 00:18:06,699
All right.

265
00:18:06,700 --> 00:18:09,084
So one thing I wanna highlight
here, which is going to be really

266
00:18:09,085 --> 00:18:11,235
important, is the primary key.

267
00:18:11,642 --> 00:18:15,227
So in terms of the primary
key, this is ScyllaDB.

268
00:18:15,505 --> 00:18:17,214
it works similarly in Cassandra.

269
00:18:17,855 --> 00:18:23,086
you the primary key will control
which queries will work, really well

270
00:18:23,087 --> 00:18:25,030
on this table and which will not.

271
00:18:25,228 --> 00:18:29,423
So in this case, we're gonna
have feature key and entity ID as

272
00:18:29,428 --> 00:18:31,598
the composite partitioning key.

273
00:18:32,183 --> 00:18:34,819
so this is going to be the
partitioning key and then item key

274
00:18:34,819 --> 00:18:36,491
will going to be the clustering key.

275
00:18:37,148 --> 00:18:41,740
A partitioning ID and a clustering
key together will be the primary

276
00:18:41,740 --> 00:18:44,162
key, in, ScyllaDB and Cassandra.

277
00:18:45,937 --> 00:18:46,453
Excuse me.

278
00:18:47,159 --> 00:18:50,475
And so basically the idea here is
that whatever columns you define

279
00:18:50,476 --> 00:18:54,514
here as the partitioning key,
you can then later filter using

280
00:18:54,517 --> 00:18:56,069
those columns in the WHERE clause.

281
00:18:56,132 --> 00:18:59,929
So on the right side, we have that
q- SELECT statement where we get

282
00:18:59,929 --> 00:19:01,727
the list items from the table.

283
00:19:02,150 --> 00:19:05,554
As you can see, we are interested
in the item key and the value.

284
00:19:06,603 --> 00:19:09,716
Value meaning or being the feature value.

285
00:19:10,386 --> 00:19:13,920
And then we are filtering by feature
key, which is the name of the feature

286
00:19:13,937 --> 00:19:18,600
that we are interested in, and the
entity ID, so we know which entity this

287
00:19:18,600 --> 00:19:21,558
feature is, related to or belongs to.

288
00:19:22,800 --> 00:19:27,056
And this is very important because w-
we won't be able to do, a different

289
00:19:27,056 --> 00:19:33,015
kind of filtering, if, after we create
the table, with this primary key.

290
00:19:33,048 --> 00:19:38,116
But usually with feature stores, you're
gonna see a primary key, or you should

291
00:19:38,117 --> 00:19:42,212
see a primary key or something similar
to this where you have the feature

292
00:19:42,212 --> 00:19:48,477
name, an entity ID, a- and you might
have a clustering column as well.

293
00:19:48,745 --> 00:19:52,454
Now what is the … What does
the clustering column do?

294
00:19:52,999 --> 00:19:57,029
you … So normally you
are defining the par…

295
00:19:57,038 --> 00:19:57,773
So the part…

296
00:19:57,817 --> 00:20:01,143
Let's start with the partitioning,
partition i- partition key first.

297
00:20:01,144 --> 00:20:05,752
So a partition key will control
which columns are used to partition

298
00:20:05,752 --> 00:20:07,090
the data across the cluster.

299
00:20:07,099 --> 00:20:10,689
So in this case, it's gonna be a
composite key, feature key and entity ID.

300
00:20:11,689 --> 00:20:17,283
Then with clustering key, it's
gonna control how the rows are

301
00:20:17,355 --> 00:20:19,207
sorted within each partition.

302
00:20:19,730 --> 00:20:26,278
So in this case, by default, if you
have w- if you don't use the, the

303
00:20:26,278 --> 00:20:32,833
clustering order by command, if you
just have a clustering key, it's

304
00:20:32,839 --> 00:20:35,484
gonna sort it in an ascending order.

305
00:20:36,268 --> 00:20:40,018
Now, i- for a feature store, that doesn't
really make sense because we are usually

306
00:20:40,035 --> 00:20:43,631
more interested in, items that are recent.

307
00:20:43,883 --> 00:20:49,287
So we want to order in a descending
o- using a des- descending method.

308
00:20:49,663 --> 00:20:54,284
So that's why we also see the clustering
order by, command there, because this

309
00:20:54,285 --> 00:21:01,503
way we are able to store the rows in
a partition in a descending order.

310
00:21:01,530 --> 00:21:06,836
And so basically, we don't need to,
re-sort the rows during query time

311
00:21:06,838 --> 00:21:12,733
because it's already stored, as, as it's
supposed to be stored on disk, by default

312
00:21:13,733 --> 00:21:14,049
All right.

313
00:21:14,106 --> 00:21:19,594
so yeah, the whole point of this sc-
design, of this, schema is that with

314
00:21:19,594 --> 00:21:24,034
one request to the database, you,
w-with one request, the database

315
00:21:24,034 --> 00:21:26,349
needs to look up one partition.

316
00:21:26,370 --> 00:21:30,264
And so it will be really fast because,
it's, the database will not spend a

317
00:21:30,264 --> 00:21:34,340
lot of time searching for the correct
partition that can deliver the data.

318
00:21:34,654 --> 00:21:37,712
Because of the partition key and
because we are using the partition

319
00:21:37,714 --> 00:21:41,922
keys in the WHERE clause, we
go straight to the partition.

320
00:21:42,175 --> 00:21:45,668
And furthermore, because of the
clustering key, we are also getting

321
00:21:45,673 --> 00:21:50,249
the most recent values without do-doing
any calculation on the fly because

322
00:21:50,251 --> 00:21:51,702
that's how it's stored on disk.

323
00:21:52,141 --> 00:21:53,237
So that's really useful.

324
00:21:53,537 --> 00:21:57,459
One extra optimization that,
ScyllaDB provides is that we have

325
00:21:57,460 --> 00:21:59,516
a shard per core architecture.

326
00:21:59,895 --> 00:22:04,430
So when, the database finds the rows
that you, are asking for in your

327
00:22:04,430 --> 00:22:09,077
SELECT statement, you are not just--
the database n- doesn't just find the

328
00:22:09,079 --> 00:22:15,648
partition, it also has dedicated resources
that can deliver those data points.

329
00:22:15,656 --> 00:22:23,177
So the s-se, specific CPU core is
responsible for specific partitions.

330
00:22:23,336 --> 00:22:26,464
And so this way it scales really
well because the CPU cores

331
00:22:26,518 --> 00:22:31,604
can, work, in parallel without,
stepping on each other's toes.

332
00:22:32,319 --> 00:22:37,117
But that's outside the scope of
this talk another thing I wanted to

333
00:22:37,117 --> 00:22:40,888
bring, into the discussion regarding
data modeling is, vector embeddings.

334
00:22:41,547 --> 00:22:47,932
So this has been, added in, in, feature
store projects in the past few years

335
00:22:47,941 --> 00:22:54,461
since, embeddings and vector data
became, popular in the past few years.

336
00:22:54,916 --> 00:22:59,460
And, there's this idea that
vector embeddings can be…

337
00:22:59,580 --> 00:23:02,104
should be treated as regular features.

338
00:23:02,493 --> 00:23:06,642
And so what that means is that you are
going to store vector embeddings in the

339
00:23:06,642 --> 00:23:09,778
same table as your regular features.

340
00:23:10,457 --> 00:23:13,631
you will query them the same
way you query regular features.

341
00:23:14,244 --> 00:23:18,578
the only difference is that you're gonna
need a new kind of index in your database

342
00:23:18,587 --> 00:23:20,642
to be able to query the vector embeddings.

343
00:23:20,645 --> 00:23:23,887
Because in practice, if you query
the vector embedding, you might

344
00:23:23,889 --> 00:23:25,861
query them by key, like the…

345
00:23:26,109 --> 00:23:28,285
In this case, we have
entity key feature name.

346
00:23:28,802 --> 00:23:32,467
So you might, query the, the, vector
embeddings the same way using the

347
00:23:32,470 --> 00:23:34,685
entity key, and the feature name.

348
00:23:34,941 --> 00:23:38,427
But, another useful aspect of
vector embeddings is that you can do

349
00:23:39,476 --> 00:23:43,422
approximate nearest neighbor search,
so you can find similar embeddings

350
00:23:43,461 --> 00:23:45,354
to the one embedding that you have.

351
00:23:46,141 --> 00:23:52,869
And, nowadays, most databases have
added some sort of support to vector

352
00:23:52,901 --> 00:23:56,796
indexes, which is good news because if
you already have a feature database,

353
00:23:57,542 --> 00:24:03,265
then you don't need to, spin up or
start a new vector database cluster just

354
00:24:03,266 --> 00:24:05,263
specifically for the vector embeddings.

355
00:24:05,684 --> 00:24:08,641
You can just create a new index
in this existing feature store

356
00:24:08,641 --> 00:24:13,592
table, and then you can start
storing and querying, vector data

357
00:24:14,592 --> 00:24:19,485
So the next thing I want to mention
is, TTL, time to live, which is a very,

358
00:24:20,108 --> 00:24:27,001
useful and, cost-effective feature in,
in, in most d- in most databases, or

359
00:24:27,002 --> 00:24:29,018
most, feature store, frameworks as well.

360
00:24:29,235 --> 00:24:35,205
basically the idea is that, as I mentioned
for feature stores, online feature stores

361
00:24:35,666 --> 00:24:42,101
where you're running real-time inference,
you don't need, like older data.

362
00:24:42,215 --> 00:24:45,630
Like after a while data, becomes, expired.

363
00:24:45,702 --> 00:24:47,078
It's not as relevant.

364
00:24:47,666 --> 00:24:50,778
and so there is this like time
series aspect, like after, after a

365
00:24:50,780 --> 00:24:52,479
while you want to just drop data.

366
00:24:52,485 --> 00:24:55,666
You don't want to include
that in your feature, vector.

367
00:24:56,155 --> 00:25:00,235
And, time to live is a, a very useful
feature that enables you to do that.

368
00:25:00,247 --> 00:25:06,014
You can set up, time to live on, … It
depends on the database how it works.

369
00:25:06,277 --> 00:25:10,455
But for example, you can set up, in
ScyllaDB you can set up a time to live,

370
00:25:10,736 --> 00:25:16,721
on, on the row, so the whole row will
get expired after 10 minutes, one day,

371
00:25:16,899 --> 00:25:19,327
one week, whatever your requirements are.

372
00:25:19,877 --> 00:25:22,755
And so basically the benefit is
that you're not storing data in

373
00:25:22,755 --> 00:25:27,213
your database, in your low-latency
database that you don't actually need.

374
00:25:27,391 --> 00:25:33,100
So you only keep, the smallest data set
possible in your low-latency database,

375
00:25:33,328 --> 00:25:38,522
and then all the other historical older
data can live in a, in a less expensive

376
00:25:38,563 --> 00:25:43,061
database environment, or some sort
of file storage or in an S3 bucket

377
00:25:43,336 --> 00:25:45,671
or using Parquet files or similar.

378
00:25:46,671 --> 00:25:47,158
All right.

379
00:25:47,260 --> 00:25:54,975
another, approach is to pre-aggregate
into, into time, buckets or tiles.

380
00:25:55,063 --> 00:25:57,201
So this is actually from, from ShareChat.

381
00:25:57,708 --> 00:26:02,020
a tile is, is a pre-computed,
count for, for a bucket.

382
00:26:02,022 --> 00:26:05,495
So for example, likes on
one post in one minute.

383
00:26:06,823 --> 00:26:11,099
And you can have, some sort of a
stream processor like Flink that

384
00:26:11,116 --> 00:26:15,570
reads the row events and then
it writes these, these, buckets.

385
00:26:16,378 --> 00:26:22,185
And then you have, a seven-day window
is then sum over about 70 tiles

386
00:26:22,679 --> 00:26:26,216
instead of a, a scan over row events.

387
00:26:27,216 --> 00:26:29,053
next thing I want to mention is caching.

388
00:26:29,526 --> 00:26:35,221
caching often, comes into the picture
when people are trying to reach,

389
00:26:35,243 --> 00:26:40,421
strict, SLA requirements and they
have an existing database that is

390
00:26:40,778 --> 00:26:45,543
Not able to reach those, low latency
numbers or maybe it's too expensive,

391
00:26:45,967 --> 00:26:48,432
and so they consider adding a cache.

392
00:26:48,709 --> 00:26:52,569
So here I want to, mention
two different ways to do this.

393
00:26:53,496 --> 00:26:57,292
So one way is that you
use an external cache.

394
00:26:57,305 --> 00:27:02,204
So you have your traditional or general
database like a persistent database

395
00:27:02,204 --> 00:27:08,072
like DynamoDB or Cassandra, Postgres,
MongoDB, ScyllaDB, some other database.

396
00:27:08,670 --> 00:27:10,693
And then you're not happy
with the latency, so you

397
00:27:10,712 --> 00:27:12,251
add a caching layer on top.

398
00:27:13,104 --> 00:27:14,621
Redis is a popular choice.

399
00:27:14,939 --> 00:27:17,630
There are other, caching
frameworks or caching tools.

400
00:27:18,005 --> 00:27:21,752
if you're using DynamoDB, you
might have had experience with DAX,

401
00:27:21,771 --> 00:27:25,541
DynamoDB Accelerator, which is a
caching layer on top of DynamoDB.

402
00:27:26,181 --> 00:27:30,741
the disadvantage with
this approach is that…

403
00:27:30,813 --> 00:27:31,879
at least two things.

404
00:27:31,895 --> 00:27:35,818
One is that, you, it's
going to increase your cost.

405
00:27:36,215 --> 00:27:39,746
So because you are, adding a
new system, you are going to,

406
00:27:40,145 --> 00:27:42,161
pay for a new system as well.

407
00:27:42,255 --> 00:27:46,165
You also have to maintain that new system,
so now you are maintaining two separate

408
00:27:46,166 --> 00:27:49,910
systems and, it can be, overwhelming.

409
00:27:50,941 --> 00:27:56,659
what, our experience is at ScyllaDB is
that a lot of teams over time decide

410
00:27:57,111 --> 00:28:02,035
to switch to a single, solution,
like a single database system.

411
00:28:02,036 --> 00:28:07,634
So instead of, using and paying a database
and then a caching layer, they would

412
00:28:07,634 --> 00:28:13,198
rather look for a database that has some
sort of an internal embedded caching

413
00:28:13,199 --> 00:28:15,927
layer, but it's a persistent database.

414
00:28:16,364 --> 00:28:22,166
And so in ScyllaDB, for example, we
have an embedded cache, that you can use

415
00:28:22,184 --> 00:28:26,194
for very strict, P99 latency, queries.

416
00:28:27,071 --> 00:28:29,678
But then at the end of the day,
it's a database that persists

417
00:28:29,699 --> 00:28:34,311
data on the disk, and you only
need to maintain one database.

418
00:28:35,311 --> 00:28:40,697
So let's look at, some of the cost
levers when you're building a feature

419
00:28:40,698 --> 00:28:42,959
store from a database perspective.

420
00:28:43,649 --> 00:28:48,144
So one, item I want to mention
is the resource, utilization.

421
00:28:49,346 --> 00:28:50,632
So when you are…

422
00:28:50,736 --> 00:28:53,533
there are, like, different pricing
models, but usually, when it comes

423
00:28:53,533 --> 00:28:59,794
to databases, but usually you pay for
the, the resources that you are using.

424
00:28:59,797 --> 00:29:03,179
The, so the CPU, the RAM, the disk.

425
00:29:04,170 --> 00:29:07,176
And, one problem with feature
stores is that sometimes,

426
00:29:07,649 --> 00:29:09,419
the workloads aren't steady.

427
00:29:09,460 --> 00:29:10,520
They aren't consistent.

428
00:29:10,531 --> 00:29:16,044
Sometimes, it's holiday season, so
traffic spikes, in, in a, in, in a

429
00:29:16,044 --> 00:29:20,067
period of, a few days or a few weeks,
and then it goes back to normal.

430
00:29:20,068 --> 00:29:20,897
Or maybe you have a…

431
00:29:21,663 --> 00:29:25,797
What we have seen with streaming
providers, streaming platforms, is that if

432
00:29:25,798 --> 00:29:31,729
you have a big sporting event, then that
can increase the traffic, on your system.

433
00:29:31,731 --> 00:29:35,295
But it- it's a spike, and so
it- it's a short-term spike.

434
00:29:36,342 --> 00:29:40,514
And basically you need, extra
capacity only for a few hours or

435
00:29:40,514 --> 00:29:45,210
few days, and the rest of the time,
you are just paying for the, for,

436
00:29:45,241 --> 00:29:47,395
for resources that you're not using.

437
00:29:47,396 --> 00:29:49,853
So this is a, this has been a problem.

438
00:29:50,288 --> 00:29:56,587
Now, in a lot of, cloud solutions,
what you have now is that, you have,

439
00:29:56,639 --> 00:30:02,726
optimizations in place where you can
only pay for what you are actually using.

440
00:30:03,540 --> 00:30:10,046
so for example, at, ScyllaDB Cloud, we
have this option to set, like a target

441
00:30:10,072 --> 00:30:16,828
utilization, let's say 80% or 90%, and
then when that's reached, the system

442
00:30:16,828 --> 00:30:19,856
